@cc [owner:ghuntley,label:security;isolation] ingress-bridge-boundaries
Only lilly-hostd runs this helper, with argv it derived itself: the client
socket (the accepted end of a connection whose peer is a socket the Lilly BEAM
created, or, detached, Audi's authenticated connection to Lilly's ingress
socket) and the guest TCP connection hostd dialled inside the Lilly network
namespace. An attached bridge runs in hostd's command cgroup and dies with
hostd; a detached bridge (ADR-0187) runs in its own transient
`lilly-ingress-<key>.scope` in lilly.slice, outlives hostd and Lilly, and is
ended by hostd killing that scope's cgroup. Run as root it first drops to the
service uid with no supplementary groups and no_new_privs; it holds no
capability, setuid bit or other descriptor. Guest and client bytes move only
between those two sockets through kernel pipes, never to stdout, stderr or a
diagnostic.

@cc [owner:ghuntley,label:verification] ingress-bridge-hegel-properties
Hegel properties MUST cover the command line against an independent oracle,
the per-direction byte accounting and the `moved` errno lattice (counts pass
through, only EAGAIN and EINTR retry, every other errno ends the bridge),
while process fixtures cover exact byte custody both ways, half-close
ordering, the client's close ending the bridge, guest loss, refusals, the
detached start handshake and the privilege drop.
